# Packages that belong to the HaikuPorts repository for this build but that the
# upstream arm64 build-packages repository does not serve, because we build them
# natively on arm64 ourselves.
#
# These must NOT be moved into repositories/HaikuPorts/arm64. That file's SHA256
# is what selects the remote package index
# (RemotePackageRepository -> "$(repoUrl)/`cat $source`/repo"), so adding a line
# to it makes the build ask the mirror for an index that was never published and
# the fetch fails with HTTP 404. Declaring them here leaves that checksum alone.
#
# Every package listed here is only ever taken from $(HAIKU_DOWNLOAD_DIR) --
# never fetched -- and is skipped (with a message) if the file is not staged
# there. So listing something that is not built yet costs nothing but the
# features that need it stay off.
#
# Versions and revisions must match the built file names exactly, e.g.
# libpng16-1.6.53-1 -> libpng16-1.6.53-1-arm64.hpkg.
#
# VENDOR INVARIANT -- applies to EVERY entry below, not just the ones whose
# comment mentions it. RemotePackageRepository (build/jam/RepositoryRules) feeds
# the staged files to `package_repo create` together with
# src/data/repository_infos/haikuports, which declares vendor "Haiku Project";
# RepositoryWriterImpl aborts the whole set if any member disagrees. The trap is
# that DeBeOS keeps two differently stamped copies of the same build: the
# pipeline hpkg pool carries the builder's "Haiku Project" stamp, while the
# published repo at packages.debene.dev is re-stamped to vendor "DeBeOS" by
# haiku-repo-publish, because a *served* repo must be single-vendor. Stage the
# published copy of any entry below and the build dies in RepositoryCache1 with
# "has unexpected vendor", skipping every image target -- that was issue 737,
# with openssl3. So stage from the pool, and stage with
# graviton/scripts/haiku-stage-local-packages, which checks the vendor of what
# it copies AND of what is already in the download dir, and refuses rather than
# leaving the failure for jam to hit an hour later.

LocalPackageRepository HaikuPorts
	: arm64
	:
	# architecture "any" packages
	:
	# repository architecture packages
	# primary architecture (arm64)

	# Image translator libraries: these turn on the libpng, jpeg, tiff and
	# jasper build features, and with them the PNG, JPEG, TIFF and JPEG2000
	# translators. The sources live in-tree (src/add-ons/translators/) and are
	# arch-neutral; they ship only when their build feature is on, which
	# BuildFeatures gates on IsPackageAvailable <lib>_devel -- true only once the
	# matching *_devel hpkg is staged into $(HAIKU_DOWNLOAD_DIR). Without these
	# entries (and the bake staging them, see graviton/pipeline/buildspecs/
	# cross-build.yml) FFilterByBuildFeatures silently strips every @libpng /
	# @jpeg / @tiff / @jasper translator from the image. The upstream arm64
	# build-packages mirror serves none of these, so DeBeOS builds and stages
	# them natively; versions match the built file names in the pool, which is
	# also where the vendor invariant at the top of this file is satisfied.
	#
	# WebPTranslator@libwebp and AVIFTranslator@libavif are deliberately NOT
	# enabled here yet:
	#   * libwebp -- the only arm64 libwebp_devel built so far exists solely as
	#     the published repo's copy, i.e. re-stamped vendor "DeBeOS", so it
	#     violates the vendor invariant above. Shipping WebP needs a
	#     "Haiku Project"-vendored libwebp(+_devel) in the pool. Unlike openssl3
	#     (issue 737) there is no pool copy to fall back on, so this one really
	#     does need a build.
	#   * libavif -- BuildFeatures gates it on IsPackageAvailable libavif1.0_devel
	#     (a >= 1.0 package); only libavif 0.9.3 (pre-1.0, package name libavif)
	#     exists for arm64. Enabling AVIF needs a native libavif >= 1.0 build.
	# Both are real package-build tasks, not a filter fix -- out of scope here.
	jasper-2.0.33-2
	jasper_devel-2.0.33-2
	libjpeg_turbo-3.1.4.1-1
	libjpeg_turbo_devel-3.1.4.1-1
	libpng16-1.6.53-1
	libpng16_devel-1.6.53-1
	tiff-4.7.0-1
	tiff_devel-4.7.0-1

	# OpenSSL/TLS: turns on the openssl build feature (build/jam/BuildFeatures),
	# so libnetapi builds BSecureSocket with real TLS instead of the
	# B_UNSUPPORTED stub -- required for https:// (any https BUrlRequest,
	# including https package repositories). Both feature gates must be
	# satisfied on arm64: gate A (IsHaikuImagePackageAdded openssl3) needs
	# openssl3 *available* so the minimum profile's by-name add lands, and gate
	# B (IsPackageAvailable openssl3_devel) needs the devel package declared.
	# The checksummed repositories/HaikuPorts/arm64 mirror serves none of these,
	# so we build and stage them ourselves. zstd_devel turns ON the zstd build
	# feature (build/jam/BuildFeatures gates it on IsPackageAvailable zstd_devel),
	# which defines ZSTD_ENABLED for BZstdCompressionAlgorithm. WITHOUT it that
	# class is a B_NOT_SUPPORTED stub, so any ZSTD-compressed hpkg -- e.g. the
	# upstream ca_root_certificates -- fails to decompress ("Failed to decompress
	# chunk data: Operation not supported"); ca_root then never activates, openssl3
	# loses its dependency, the /boot/system volume goes inconsistent (intermittent
	# first-boot stall), and TLS cert verification has no CA store (https =
	# "Operation not allowed"). zstd (runtime) alone only satisfies openssl3's
	# lib:libzstd>=1.5.6; the reader needs the build feature, i.e. zstd_devel.
	# Staging openssl3 WITHOUT the zstd runtime does not surface as a solver
	# error, which is what you would expect: the downloaded mirror index does
	# advertise zstd-1.5.6-2, so the solver resolves lib:libzstd to it happily
	# and build_haiku_image then tries to fetch it from the arm64 mirror, which
	# 404s (measured on issue 737). So openssl3 and zstd are staged as a pair.
	# NOTE: openssl3 and zstd must NOT also be force-added via <hg-pool> in
	# graviton/ssh/UserBuildConfig -- the profile's by-name add installs one
	# instance, so a second (file) instance makes the image solver report
	# "would need to uninstall package openssl3".
	openssl3-3.5.7-1
	openssl3_devel-3.5.7-1
	zstd-1.5.6-2
	zstd_devel-1.5.6-2
	# zstd_source provides the zstd .c/.h tree at develop/sources/zstd-1.5.6-2/
	# sources, which the kernel (src/system/kernel/lib/zstd) and EFI boot loader
	# compile into kernel_libzstd.a via [ BuildFeatureAttribute zstd : sources ].
	# Without it the cross-build fails "don't know how to make ...zstd_common.c".
	# Not served by the upstream arm64 mirror, so DeBeOS builds+stages it.
	zstd_source-1.5.6-2

	# HaikuWebKit: turns on the webkit build feature, which is what makes
	# src/apps/webpositive compile at all (it exits its Jamfile early unless
	# FIsBuildFeatureEnabled webkit). Built natively on arm64 from our fork of
	# the recipe, with -DUSE_AVIF=OFF -- libavif >= 16 needs librav1e, which is
	# Rust, and there is no Rust for arm64 Haiku.
	haikuwebkit-1.10.0-2
	haikuwebkit_devel-1.10.0-2

	# Basic userland the lean base image otherwise lacks (#38). `which` resolves
	# commands on PATH; provisioning scripts and recipe build steps assume it.
	# The upstream arm64 build-packages mirror serves no `which`, so DeBeOS
	# builds and stages it here (dev-util/which; version matches the other
	# arches' current haikuports build). Skipped with a message -- not a build
	# error -- until the hpkg is staged in $(HAIKU_DOWNLOAD_DIR). (awk is
	# covered separately: `gawk` resolves straight from the arm64 mirror, so it
	# needs no local entry and adding one would collide with that instance.)
	which-2.21-6

	# Native MIDI backend for the MIDI Kit (#50). build/jam/BuildFeatures gates
	# the `fluidlite` build feature on BOTH fluidlite_devel AND libvorbis_devel
	# being available; only then does src/kits/midi build libmidi.so (it links
	# [ BuildFeatureAttribute libvorbis : library ] and fluidlite's static lib,
	# and returns early otherwise). With the feature enabled, libmidi.so@fluidlite
	# survives FFilterByBuildFeatures and is baked into @minimum, so ports that
	# link the MIDI Kit 1 shared lib -- e.g. sdl2_mixer's native_midi_haiku
	# backend, which links -lmidi -- resolve libmidi.so. The upstream arm64
	# build-packages mirror serves none of these, so DeBeOS builds and stages
	# them natively (media-sound/fluidlite, media-libs/libvorbis; versions match
	# the x86_64/riscv64 HaikuPorts entries). fluidlite-1.0.9-3 needed
	# -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 to configure under CMake 4.x. Skipped
	# with a message -- not a build error -- until staged in $(HAIKU_DOWNLOAD_DIR).
	fluidlite-1.0.9-3
	fluidlite_devel-1.0.9-3
	libvorbis-1.3.7-1
	libvorbis_devel-1.3.7-1
	;
